You are not a Frenchman. And therefore, you know, there's only one way you're headed. And that's, you're getting on your tumbril. This is the kind of blueprint for the 20th century totalitarian states. But I guess to defend Robespierre, he doesn't know that. He is still negotiating the contour. of this kind of new dispensation, which is something very novel and potentially chilling, as you said. But I think you have to believe in Ross-Pierre's incorruptibility.

124
01:00:42.000 --> 01:00:58.000
He does think he's doing the right thing. But that's the chilling thing, though, isn't it, about Rosspere? He's totally sincere. Yeah. If he was here now, he would say, you don't understand. I am going to build a better world. I genuinely am interested in virtue, because virtue without terror is nothing. That's exactly what he thinks.

125
01:00:58.000 --> 01:01:21.000
That is exactly what he thinks. It's telling, I think, people keep falling ill so often in this series. It's a sign of how overwrought Rob Speer is that he gives the speech and he immediately falls ill, overworked, stressed. He seems, I mean, everybody has it, but he does seem especially prone to burnout. And he retreats for almost a month to the DuPlai family's house,

126
01:01:21.000 --> 01:01:53.000
which is where he lodges, which you described before. Now, while he's away, the pressure carers on building, France is now mobilising meritorily on an unprecedented scale in European history. 700,000 men in uniform. The whole economy is basically being put to work to feed and supply the army. And that means that everything else is deteriorating. So on the 23rd of February, government agents report to the committees. The situation in Paris is really alarming.

127
01:01:53.000 --> 01:02:15.000
There are shops that are completely empty. The butchers are all closed. People are fighting in the streets for food. And when merchants arrive in the city with supplies in the countryside, they're being attacked by mobs of women who are scrabbling to get all this bread or vegetables or whatever. So it's like 1789 all over again. It is like 1789 or over again. But the committee are very unsympathetic.

128
01:02:15.000 --> 01:02:35.000
So the committee actually say, well, if the public, you know, they're winging about no bread, they must cultivate Spartan virtues. George Couton says, what are they complaining about? All they need is a single scrap of me today. If I had the police power in my hands, I would make privation, the order of the day. Slogans for a happier France.

129
01:02:35.000 --> 01:03:03.000
Of course, the person who's benefiting from this is Abert on the ultra-radical wing. Because by early February, Abert has turned his rhetoric up to 11. He is running these absolutely ferocious editorials saying, you know, Tavern, keepers, butchers, grocers, farmers, get them all to the guillotine. If they can't provide for the people at Paris, we will, you know, punish them until they do. There are protests in the streets.

130
01:03:03.000 --> 01:03:30.000
There's this sort of sense that we've had so often the revolution of momentum, of tension, of moving towards an inevitable showdown. And that moment seems to come on the 4th of March 1794. Abert goes to the Cordilliers Club, founded ironically by Dantan, and he says, You know, heads have got a roll. I've got a list here of 73 deputies. These are people who supported the Girondas. They've got to go. Damien's got to go.

131
01:03:30.000 --> 01:03:51.000
There's a load of moderates and weaklings. They're traitors. They've got to go. And at the end of this meeting, they take a black veil and they put it over the framed picture of the Declaration of the Rights of Man. And they say, we are not going to withdraw it until the people have recovered their sacred rights. And everybody understands, this is the cue.

132
01:03:51.000 --> 01:04:38.000
the signal for a new insurrection, a new attack on the convention, effectively, a new son-coulot coup. But the difference between this and previous ones is that this one fizzles out almost immediately. Because they call to the sections of Paris, the neighbourhoods, but only one of them, the Cordilliers' home one, is the only one that answers the call. The Paris Commune, the city government doesn't call out the National Guard, there is a sense, I think, that Paris is exhausted. That basically people are sick of grabbing their halberds or whatever and, you know, taking to the streets. Is this the first time that an attempt to outflank the kind of establishment on the left fails?

133
01:04:38.000 --> 01:05:06.000
Yes, it is. That radicalism up till now has always seemed to have the wind in its sails. And this is the first time that the wind doesn't blow the sails. Yes, it is. That's what Abe's fate. absolutely denotes. It's like this far and no further. The radical energies of the revolution have reached their kind of natural limit, I would say. So amid all this, amid all this kind of chaos, the convention votes to renew the powers of the Committee of Public Safety for another month.

134
01:05:06.000 --> 01:05:44.000
And that same evening, Robespier returns from his sickbed for the first time in weeks. And he goes to the Committee of Public Safety. It's a momentous sort of late-night meeting. San Joust has brought along a big report on the rival factions, a denunciation of the factions. So I want to read this to the convention tomorrow. The rest of the committee say, fine, go for it. So the next day, the 13th of March, Sanjust gets up in the tribune of the convention, and he reads out what he calls, a report on the factions of foreign inspiration and on the conspiracy plotted by them to destroy representative government by corruption and to starve Paris.

135
01:05:44.000 --> 01:06:07.000
And San Jose says to the convention, Sanjutor is a very compelling speaker. People are frightened of Sanjutor as a speaker because he's so good at it. He says all of this that we see on the streets right now, the food shortages, the political opposition, the sense of mounting chaos. This is part of a vast conspiracy masterminded from London, a conspiracy so vast that it's penetrated the clubs

136
01:06:07.000 --> 01:06:23.000
and it's working through the factions, people who are moderates, people who are extremists, they're all pretending, they are working for our enemies. Every faction is criminal because it works to divide our citizens. And as Palmer says in his book, it's not the voice of revolution.

137
01:06:23.000 --> 01:06:48.000
It's not the voice of rebellion. This is the language of men in power. So it's the language of authority stamping out dissent. And that evening, the committee makes its move. Ebert and his key collaborators are arrested and taken off to prison. alongside, and this is a very reign of terror trick, this bizarre collection of people

138
01:06:48.000 --> 01:07:03.000
that they will just throw in to try to give the appearance of a conspiracy. So to quote Palmer on this, the people arrested included a Dutch banker, a French general, a hairdresser, a bookseller, a tobacconist, a doctor, a surgeon's apprentice, an ex-peasant woman,

139
01:07:03.000 --> 01:07:20.000
and someone described as a man of letters. String up the man of letters. Definitely. Someone described as a man of letters is a very nice touch. It's like in the old days, like in the old days of my mid-market newspaper, tub thumping, when I used to refer to people,

140
01:07:20.000 --> 01:07:41.000
I disapproved of self-styled intellectuals. Anyway, these people are all dragged in front of the tribunal. They're accused of being a foreign conspiracy. The trial begins on the 22nd of March. A bear is accused of trying to undermine the economy, but there's a tax on shopkeepers. Abe can't believe this. Yeah.

141
01:07:41.000 --> 01:07:58.000
He's the toast of the streets. He's a friend of the people. Yeah. Abeau is completely stunned by this. And he doesn't take it seriously, I think. He thinks the streets will rise and rescue him. And this doesn't happen at all. And when he's proclaimed guilty on the 24th of March and his sentence to death,

142
01:07:58.000 --> 01:08:11.000
Abeau is stunned. He can't believe it. Don't you know who I am? Exactly. He's taken away. He's taken straight to the guillotine. There's a massive crowd. and people are so excited about this.

143
01:08:11.000 --> 01:08:28.000
I mean, A-Bear, the man who wanted to guillotine everybody else, he's getting killetons. And he disgraces himself. He's a shambles. He's no Marion Twinnett. So he does not behave with, yeah, the dignity of the ex-Queen. No. So one man in the crowd, the government agents reported to the committee is what was said.

144
01:08:28.000 --> 01:08:53.000
So there's brilliant quotes. One man in the crowd apparently said of Abe and his collaborators, they died like cowards without balls. And another one sounding very Trumpy, And I have to say, said, we thought that A bear would have more courage, but he died like a loser. Thank you for your attention to this matter. Yeah, he died like a dog. Anyway, that's the end of ABA.

145
01:08:53.000 --> 01:09:29.000
Now, as you said, Tom, this is a very significant moment in the revolution, because it's the first time you've had authority versus the streets, and authority has definitively won. And there's a sense, I think, now, okay, it's clear that where the revolution is, you know, is not going to go. You know, it's not going to tip over into this sort of ultra-populism. However, the other implication of this, the committees wanted to hold what they saw as the line between the two factions. They've basically crushed one. What are they going to do about the other, which is Dantan and his allies?

146
01:09:29.000 --> 01:09:59.000
So now we come back to the issue of Dantan and Robespierre. As you said at the beginning, it's seen as this kind of titanic duel between these two completely different personalities, struggle for the soul of the revolution, all of this, a struggle for power between two implacable antagonists. But that's really not right. Rob Spierre, I think, does not want to kill Dantan. That's why it takes so long to do it. And he genuinely, I think, hopes that Dantan will come round.

147
01:09:59.000 --> 01:10:21.000
So if you've seen the film, in the film from the 1980s, this is very celebrated scene where Dantan and Robespier have a kind of reconciliation. dinner. Organised by Dantan in the film, which I think is not correct. And Dantan is like stuffing himself with oysters. He's like a man at the rest of his history as Christmas dinner. Wine, port, foie gras, you name it. It's all flower.

148
01:10:21.000 --> 01:10:48.000
Exactly. But Robert Sparrow is just sitting there, chilly, doesn't want to eat anything. Actually, in the written sources, most of them say that they had this meeting, Dantan was very emotional, and some of them say Dantan burst into tears. Those sources tend to come from the later 19th century. And I think what historians think is they probably, what probably happened was it might not have been a dinner. It might have been a dinner, we don't know.

149
01:10:48.000 --> 01:11:18.000
But they must have had one or more meetings in the last week in March. Robespierre saying to Dantan, could you give us your support? Could you back the committees? You know, surely we can find a way through this. And Dantantan not playing ball, saying, no, I don't agree with you. I think the terror has to end. I think, I mean, I remember that Rob Spier, the stories that Dantan cried, that Robespier saw this as a maligned tactic on the part of Dantan to make him feel bad.

150
01:11:18.000 --> 01:11:36.000
Yeah, exactly. But they're so emotional. They're always crying, aren't they, the French revolutionary leaders? I mean, I don't think it's unreasonable by Dantan. Anyway, the truth is, the sources on this are so vague. It's impossible to know. What we do know is that by the end of March, Rob Spierreier has made up his mind that Dantan has to go. It is clear to Robespier, Dantéier,

151
01:11:36.000 --> 01:12:09.000
Dantant is not going to abandon his campaign against the terror. And actually, D'Amoulin has prepared a new issue of the Vue d'Aulé and it's seen as so damning about the Committee of Public Safety that the printers are refusing to touch it. So, as Robespier sees it, I mean, people always tell the story from D'Anthor's perspective, completely understandably and not Robespier's, but just from Robespier's perspective. Robespier's perspective is D'Onton is going to destroy the revolution. The terror is what's keeping the revolution going and Dantan is going to destroy it

152
01:12:09.000 --> 01:12:22.000
and he's surrounded himself with a load of kind of total mediocrities and actors. Plus, he's massively corrupt and he's always taking bungs. Yeah. And you can't have that in a virtuous republic. No.

153
01:12:22.000 --> 01:12:47.000
So the last days of March, Robespier and Saint-Jus to prepare the indictment. The case, as we've said, writes itself, right, the corruption. We know that Dantan took dodgy money. Robespier has all these allegations of massive conspiracies and things. And, you know, Dantan has been unguarded and he has said things to Robespierre that most people would laugh off, but Robespierre, you know, he's not a funster.

154
01:12:47.000 --> 01:13:22.000
So when Dantan said to him, they were talking about virtue, and Dantan said to Robespierre, I know no virtue greater than that which I practice every night with my wife. Yeah. Robespier does not smile at this. He thinks this is disgraceful. And Saint-Ju thinks it's disgraceful as well. and in the speech that he will give against Dantan, he cites that specifically and clearly does not take it as a joke in the slightest. And I think the lesson is it's dangerous to make jokes when Rosfiel and San Jusseuzeuze are on the scene. Completely. So late on the evening of the 29th of March, the drama is set in train.

155
01:13:22.000 --> 01:13:44.000
So there's a joint meeting of the Committee of Public Safety and the Committee of General Security. Sanjoust presents them with his indictment. He says, I'm going to read this in the convention tomorrow. and then I'm going to call for Dantan's arrest. And the men from the General Security Committee, basically the political police, Vardier and Amar, they say, are you mad?

156
01:13:44.000 --> 01:14:00.000
Dantan is a brilliant speaker. If you read this out in the convention, Dantan will reply, he will destroy you, and then you'll look like a fool. You arrest him first, get him safely locked up, then denounce him. Saint-Just is very offended by this,

157
01:14:00.000 --> 01:14:16.000
by the thought that they don't rate his rhetorical ability, compare with Dantons. He basically has a strop. He throws his hat in the fire and then storms out. That is a strob. Yeah, it's a massive sulk. So he storms out. And actually, all the other members of the Committee of Public Safety say,

158
01:14:16.000 --> 01:14:33.000
say, do you know what, you're right? We probably should arrest him first. So they agreed they'll do that. They drew up the warrant. One by one, they sign it. You can see this in the film, very famous scene. One member of the Committee of General Security says he won't sign it. And also a member of the Committee of Public Safety, Robert Lindy,

159
01:14:33.000 --> 01:14:52.000
who's in charge of supplying the army. And Lindy says, my job is to provide for our citizens not to execute our patriots. And that little hint there of a fracture within the Committee of Public Safety between the people who are getting stuff done, Lindy, Kano and people like that,

160
01:14:52.000 --> 01:15:12.000
and then the people who love talking about virtue and organizing executions, that's going to become more pronounced in a couple of months. Anyway, this meeting breaks up. So it's now the early hours the 30th of March. I think it's pretty clear that in the next few hours, rumours spread and somebody tipped d'Anne and de Malin off.

161
01:15:12.000 --> 01:15:31.000
There were various accounts of what was said. Dantant supposedly, one version said that he was told flee the city, and he said, no, they won't move against me, they wouldn't dare. D'Onton was cocky. Another version he's told to emigrate, and he said, no, I will never leave France. You cannot carry your homeland on the soles of your shoes, a nice line.

162
01:15:31.000 --> 01:16:10.000
and there are stories that de Milan went to see his old classmate, Maximilian Rospier, went to his house but was turned away from the door and said, I am lost. If he won't see me, I am lost. Anyway, that night, the 30th, they are arrested, they're taken to the Luxembourg prison, they're going to face trial by the Revolutionary Tribunal. We don't have any accounts of the scene when they were arrested, unfortunately. But we do know that the next day, the 31st, the news spread very much. quickly. It was a massive sensation. A police agent reported, people are stunned and don't know what to think. And in the convention, a scene of total chaos. So one of Dantons' allies

163
01:16:10.000 --> 01:16:47.000
rushes into the convention. He says, unbelievable. Dantan's been arrested. Citizens, I declare that Dantons is as pure as I am. That's an unwise thing to say. Yeah, I know. God. I mean, else for trouble, why don't you? Risky. So there's Tumel in the hall. This guy who broke the news, he's called Lejeant. he says, they should bring in the prisoners, bring in Dantan Damiena, let them face their accusers, bring in the committee, let's have this out. And Rob Spier forces his way through the shouting throng of people. He is described as pale with anger and seemingly impelled by the sense of a great danger.

164
01:16:47.000 --> 01:17:15.000
And he gets up there and he says to the convention, from his perspective, not unreasonably, we've arrested so many people. Why does Dantan get special treatment? You know, we should treat the people we arrest equally. And he says to them, do you not have the courage to break a false idol? And then he is followed onto the tribunal by Saint-Just. The archangel of death, icy kind of recital of the charges. Danton was working with the foreign plot.

165
01:17:15.000 --> 01:17:30.000
Dantan was taking money. And if you doubt it, look what's happening to France. Look at the shortages. Look at the collapse of that currency. Look at the hoarding of goods. Look at the unrest in the provinces. This is all the foreign plot. the men we are denouncing were never patriots.

166
01:17:30.000 --> 01:18:05.000
They were aristocrats more adroit and cunning than those at Koblenz, i.e. the emigres. Yeah, their maxims were those of Cataline. Exactly. So they've gone from being Cicero, they've become Cataline. So this does the trick. The Robs-Piers-S-S-Just combo, which often works, the convention fall into line. And so it is that two days later, on the 2nd of April, Dantant and Desmilae are led into the Pallé de Justice for trial by the tribunal. And as with Ebert, the committee have mudded the waters by throwing in a load of random people,

167
01:18:05.000 --> 01:18:33.000
bankers and things. There's a Dutchman, the Belgian, there's two Moravians, there's a Dane and there's a Spaniard. I mean, to be fair, everyone hates a foreign banker. They do, exactly. I thought you were going to say everyone hates a Dutchman. No, I love a Dutchman. You love the Dutch. And Fabre and Chabot, they're also there. So the two blocs had gone to Robespier to save themselves with their plot stories. Yeah. They failed to do so. And the point, of course, is throwing so many people in this to create the sense of conspiracy. And from the start, Dantan's in great form.

168
01:18:33.000 --> 01:18:53.000
He says to his fellow prisoners, provided they let us speak at length, I am sure of routing my accusers. I don't think he thinks he's going to get away. He thinks he's just going to humiliate them and they will execute him, but he will go down fighting and history will reward him. He'll leave a name to posterity. Completely. At this point, I think, I do have to say,

169
01:18:53.000 --> 01:19:11.000
Dantan is very confused in my mind with Winston Churchill because I think this is exactly how Churchill would have behaved. You know, the sort of the bumpuousness, the arrogance. Looking to history as his judge. Completely. Yeah. I mean, this is so Churchillian. They ask him, the trial, yeah, when he's sort of checking into the prison or something,

170
01:19:11.000 --> 01:19:34.000
what's your name and address? And he says, I will soon be living nowhere. As for my name, you'll find it in the pantheon of history. I mean, that's amazing. Yeah. But de Moulat is even less modern. is, isn't he? Yes, he is. I mean, Damon, to be fair, who's often described as this sort of immature weakling. They ask him for his age, and he says, I'm 33, the same age as the

171
01:19:34.000 --> 01:20:03.000
Soncoulot Jesus Christ when he stood before Pontius Pilots. I mean, you can see how, basically, if you were standing next to Donsoe, you'd be, you'd raise your game, wouldn't you? You'd raise your repartee game. And actually what follows, the trial. The trial is one of the great set pieces in all French history. So it's held in the palidogistis. It's packed. The Galleries are rammed with spectators. Massive anticipation. The prosecutor, I mean, you couldn't make this up. The prosecutor is Damonland's distant cousin.

172
01:20:03.000 --> 01:20:30.000
He's a guy called Fouquier-Tanville. And he's always described as the villain of the piece, that one of Stalin's prosecutors. But actually, I think that's a bit unfair. Most historians say Fouquier is actually quite a conscientious lawyer and a stickler for details, methodical, serious and stuff. and he completely fails to keep order. So people are shouting all the time from the galleries.

173
01:20:30.000 --> 01:20:52.000
Danton, who's a very big man, is just treating this as a stage. He's like a kind of bull tossing dogs left, right and center. Completely. And he's so loud, everyone says he's got this Karen's voice. He's so loud that people are standing in the street outside, outside the building and they can still hear him. He says this on day one.

174
01:20:52.000 --> 01:21:18.000
men like me cannot be bought. On their brows are imprinted in in a faceable characters, the seal of liberty, the genius of republicanism, and I am accused of having crawled at the feet of vile despots. And the court president tries to cut him off by tinkling this little bell. And Dantan says, The voice of a man who has to defend both his life and honour must vanquish the sound of your little bell.

175
01:21:18.000 --> 01:21:39.000
So that's day one. He's warming up. Day two is the turn of the defence. So Foucair's presented the indictment. At this point, Don't I is just an absolutely titanic form. He doesn't bother looking at the jury or at the judges. He does all of his remarks to the spectators. And he says, my friends, you will judge me when you have heard me.

176
01:21:39.000 --> 01:21:58.000
My voice will be heard, not just by you, but by all of France. And the world. And the world, exactly. He says, you know, where are my accusers? Where is Rob Spear? Where are the committees? that cowards who slander me but do not dare to attack me to my face. And at the end of the day, he ends with these absolutely magnificent lines.

177
01:21:58.000 --> 01:22:24.000
If ever I'm on trial for some driving offence or something, I'd like to think I'd go down like this. During the last two days, the court has got to know Danton. Tomorrow, he hopes to sleep in the bosom of glory. He has never asked for pardon. You will see him fly to the scaffold with his usual serenity and the calm of a clear conscience. Do you know, I almost want you to be arrested for a traffic offence.

178
01:22:24.000 --> 01:22:40.000
Thanks. I can hear you deliver that peroration. Do you? The reality would be so banal. I'm so sorry. I'm a bit tired. So, for the authorities, this is a terrible embarrassment. And it's actually getting worse because at the beginning of day three,

179
01:22:40.000 --> 01:23:06.000
Dantan says, I would like to call a long list of witnesses in my defense. And he has a huge list of deputies from the convention and even members of of the Committee of Public Safety. And the judges say, no, you can't call all of these people. And the crowds start hooting and shouting at them. And Fouquet-Tanville, the prosecutor, who is described by one witness's affolet, which means panicked, he scribbles and notes the Committee of Public Safety. And he says, look, this is totally out of control now.

180
01:23:06.000 --> 01:23:47.000
If we don't change the rules, I cannot guarantee that we will get Danton's conviction. And Sanjoust rushes to the convention, and he says, we have a national emergency. I have to say this is shameless from Saint-Just. We've all been talking about his, you know, their rigid principles and their belief in their own virtue and they really sincere and all this. But this, I cannot be sincere. This is cynical. San Jus says, Dantan's allies in the prisons are planning a mass breakout and they're going to murder members of the convention. And actually, Daminglein's wife, Lucille, is in on it. She's one of the organizers. That's not gallant, is it? It's not good from Sajus. And Sanjouin,

181
01:23:47.000 --> 01:24:31.000
just goes on to say, look at the way Dantan is behaving in the courtroom, shouting, dominating all of this. Doesn't that prove he's guilty? What innocent man ever revolted against the law? I mean, a chilling sentiment. Basically defend yourself. It proves you're guilty. And the Convention approves an emergency decree that the prosecutors can remove defendants from the courtroom if their behavior insults the justice of the nation. That same day, by the way, the Committee of General security issues of warrant for Lucille de Moulin's arrest as well, to DeMille's wife. Now, she in the interim had written Rob Spierre a very moving letter, reminding him of their old friendship and how important he'd been to their family.

182
01:24:31.000 --> 01:25:07.000
She said to him, do you think the people will respect you for destroying your friends? Do you think they will bless somebody who doesn't care about a widow's tears or an orphan's plight? And she even went to Rob Spire's house that Robespier refused to see. I mean, the thing is that for Robespierre, this is the index of his virtue, because he is genuinely distraught at the thought of them dying. Yeah, I'm such a kind person, I'm killing my friends. Well, but, you know, who would you betray your friends or your country? He's betraying his friends. Yeah, exactly. So in the meantime, Fouquet-Tanville now has what he needs. The next morning,

183
01:25:07.000 --> 01:25:46.000
the 5th of April, Danton and Coe are removed from the court. They're dragged out, and as they're dragged out, Dantantz shouts furiously. He says, we're going to be judged without being heard. And then he says very mockingly, you know, go on, take us to the scaffold. Fugier says to the jury, I believe you've heard all you need to. Can you make your decision? The sentence is, of course, death. As they're waiting in the Luxembourg prison, Dantan seems rueful. He's actually quite wry. He says, funny thing is none of those people know anything about government. If I left my balls to Rob Spierreau and my legs to coot on. the Committee of Public Safety might last a bit longer.

184
01:25:46.000 --> 01:26:12.000
I think that's a very good line. Damoulin, however, is crushed at the thought of parting from his wife Lucille. He doesn't know at this point that she has been arrested. And he writes to this last letter, very affecting, one of the most well-known letters in French history. My Lucille, my darling, despite my torment, I believe there is a God. One day I will see you again. Adieu, lulu. Adieu, my life, my soul, my goddess on earth.

185
01:26:12.000 --> 01:26:34.000
I feel the shores of life receding before me. I see you again, Lucille. I see my arms around you. My tied hands embrace you. My severed head rests its eyes on you. But she never actually gets to read it because she has now been locked up as well. So late that afternoon, they are taken to the guillotine. Dusk is falling.

186
01:26:34.000 --> 01:26:58.000
You described the scene, the eyewitness account. Des Molyne, just before he was executed, was told that his wife had been arrested and he was devastated. He fought with the executionist on the scaffold until the shirt had almost been ripped from his body and they have to force him into the guillotine. And then d'Anton goes last. He cuts this incredibly defiant figure, that amazing last line.

187
01:26:58.000 --> 01:27:12.000
Don't forget to show my head to the people. It'll be worth it. You know, a very fitting epitaph. And finally, Lucille de Milance, she was guillotined a week later and she wrote a very affecting last letter to her mother before she died. Good night, dear Mamma.

188
01:27:12.000 --> 01:27:32.000
A tear falls from my eyes. It is for you. I am going to sleep in the tranquility of innocence. So that's that. Danton's dead and Robespierre and the committees have won. And on the face of it, Robespierre now appeals unassailable. And he and his allies have crushed their critics

